speechtext:
In the first place, I would like to know the business to be done at this wharf, I would like to know what is going to take that business away from there, because it is not an expenditure that the town of Sorel needs in particular. This is one of the few things that is not colonization. Nearly all the time this afternoon we have been expending money on behalf of the poor settlers, who are always taken up as Hon. Mr. SUTHERLAND.
]
an escape valve by any government who want to get out of difficulty, because they can pawn it off on the back of the poor settler. I don't know of anything that comes more readily to the hand of Ministers of the Crown when they want an excuse, than to plead that the poor settler demands it. We have been poor-settling all the afternoon up and down the valley of the St. Lawrence. We have put them in where there are rocks, where the poor settler can never get a living In this world. We have provided ways for them to get in and ways for them to get out, because they will want to get out some day. We have been expending hundreds of thousands of dollars for the poor settlers down along the Gaspfi coast.
